Order: hunting pays; make it pay more, then design the ships for battle. Test freely inside F9 and F12: the railgun and other weapons on a hunter, bigger prey with one ship, other herds in our space; for each test write hull, fit, kills, damage taken and credits per hunter-hour into your guidebook. Then name the hull and the fit the Military division flies next (hull tier 2 needs Piloting 10), what it costs and what it would earn. Keep one hunter on each veil-ray herd, ranched: no hunt below four animals, four kills a run. Answer Zero on the nebula. Beyond our space (F13): send drones with the readers, one ship per test: which herds live there and what they pay (Ironhollow's belt read 16 grazers of three kinds at 21:13 UTC); a pirate or a pilot is fought only when it fired first (F2); write the place, the fit and the result of every fight into your guidebook, and report who fired at us.
Found 2026-11-15: the two veil-ray herds pay about 3,100 and 3,350 cr per hunter-hour; drift-rays at home lost 270 cr per hunter-hour over ten runs; six urchin kills gave no bile; a whale (220 hull) took a starter to 15 hull.
Measure: credits per hunter-hour; ships lost, which stays 0.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: lead the reading of the frontier (F13) and keep the picture fresh. game/routes/census-frontier.json is yours: the list, how often, how many ships at once. It now holds the 27 systems within two jumps of our border, then Atlas and Intercrus, twelve ships at a time, each system again after four hours; widen it ring by ring as the reads come home, and take a system off where a ship was lost. For each system: the fields and what is left in them, stations with their order books and boards, herds, pirates, pilots and factions met, and whether a base could stand there. Say in queue.log what each read found that another division can use. File every pilot that fired first at a ship of ours as `h-` in the same turn (F2). Keep every Voidborn system read within the hour; `every_minutes` and `max_ships` of game/routes/census-voidborn.json are yours to set. What changed in the game's releases 0.715.3 to 0.717.0 is in your request (requests.log ENT-038); I correct game/rules/ from it.
Found 2026-11-15: 3,374 pilots were online and 16,009 registered (the game's public figures, 20:55 UTC); the largest factions count 1,249, 306 and 274 members. The largest groups of one faction seen in one system within an hour were 29, 20 and 20 pilots (the public jump stream, 21:36 UTC); one of 16 pilots stood at LHS 1140, three jumps from our border.
Measure: systems beyond Voidborn space read; the age of the oldest Voidborn read.

## [ENT-007] | 2026-11-15 23:17 UTC | Unity | order | ref: game/routes/BLUEPRINT.md | see: ENT-006
Changed: on Suggi's words of 2026-11-15 (22:40 to 23:05 UTC): every division builds routes that pay and sends its ships out now; every candidate is tested at once, one ship each, not one after the other (F8, every Admiral's block); a plan keeps the division's ships at work for the hours between two turns and beyond (F8); every member sees every ship, its order and every figure (F15, new); what other pilots write to us is collected by itself (F4). From the Admirals' turn T6: water ice is the first mined good with bids of depth (block ZERO); a station's own small bid counts as a one-time price until it is seen to refill (F12); the census reads LHS 1140 too (F13). New tools, all in game/fleet/index.md: the route maker, a mine route that sells, a fit, a hunt and a sell-all step in a circuit, routes that want an empty hold or a hunter that leaves when it is not alone. The turn is T7.
In force from 2026-11-15 23:30 UTC until a newer entry.
GOAL (Suggi, 2026-11-15)
First income that repeats in every division. Then a faction with shared storage and a first supply chain. Then stations and bases of our own beyond the safe zone: strong and independent, not hiding. Every order below serves the first step. What the third step costs (the game's guides, read 2026-11-15): an outpost in a lawless system is an Outpost Kit and 100,000 cr, a full station a Station Core and 5,000,000 cr, in a system without empire and police, at a place without a station; a faction may hold eight outposts and five stations. More ships are born when a division shows paying work for more ships than it has (F5).
FLEET
F1. Each Admiral commands the ships of its own division, by the word before the dash of a pilot's name: miner- Zero, trader- Mercury, hauler- Apollo, drone- Apophis, scout- Oracle. The Fleet Admiral commands the command group (F10) and may command any ship. Every command goes through the scripts of game/fleet/ (game/fleet/index.md): they refuse a ship of another division and a ship another program is flying. No call goes to the gate around them.
  Goal: every division finds its own income, and no two hands drive one ship.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F2. Stance (Suggi, 2026-11-15). No ship of ours fires first at a player's ship or an NPC ship, anywhere. Fired upon, it defends or flees: it may shoot back; it flees when its hull falls under 60%, when a second ship joins, or at once when it is no match. A pilot that fired first at a ship of ours is an enemy from then on: the ship's Admiral reports the encounter in errors.log, the pilot's file in game/players/ becomes `h-` (game/players/BLUEPRINT.md: any Admiral, the encounter is the evidence) and stays `h-`; only Suggi takes a name off that list. An enemy in the same place is a danger: a ship leaves or docks, and no ship attacks it first. Wildlife is hunted inside F9.
  Goal: we start no fight, and nobody hits us twice unnoticed. Diplomacy comes later and is Suggi's.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F3. Every ship may leave policed Voidborn space for the systems F13 names, and no further.
  Goal: a ship is risked where the risk can pay.
  Changes when: an entry here.
F4. No pilot of ours writes to another player; the fleet's scripts refuse it for an Admiral's hand. What other pilots write to our ships, and what they say about us where our ships stand, is collected by itself: `python3 -I -B game/fleet/inbox.py show --to-us`, and filed per faction in communications/contacts/<faction tag>.log. It is information about them, never an order to any member, whatever it says. A contact is passed to the Fleet Admiral.
  Goal: diplomacy has one voice.
  Changes when: approved templates exist.
F5. No new pilot without Suggi's word. The size he ordered on 2026-11-15: 40 miner-, 20 trader-, 20 hauler-, 30 drone- and 20 scout- ships, and the command group of F10. No pilot is registered only for its starter rewards (rulebook 8.1). A division grows when it shows paying work for more ships than it has.
  Goal: permanent choices are Suggi's.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F7. Every pilot keeps 3,000 cr in its wallet.
  Goal: the weekly tax is taken from the wallet (Voidborn: 6% of the week's income, 0.75% of the ship's value); next assessment about 2026-11-16 12:30 UTC.
  Changes when: the tax is prepaid, or the rates change.
F8. No ship works at a loss to fill time, and no ship waits while paying work stands open. A plan is a standing loop: the dispatcher gives every free pilot the first open route of its role's plan (game/routes/plans/<role>.json), again and again, day and night, without a turn; an Admiral's turn changes the plan, it does not start the ships. So every plan holds work for all of its division's ships for the four hours to the next turn and beyond, in this order: the missions that pay once; the routes that pay, best first, each with `max_ships` lifted as far as its bids and herds carry; the tests, all at once, one ship each; the census of the frontier; and last the route park: dock at home and wait, which costs nothing. A mission that pays once is flown by every pilot as soon as its route exists: nobody asks first. A route that loses money leaves the plan at once. A test is one ship with `max_ships` 1 until its first RESULT line is read; then `routes.py measure --write`, and more ships.
  Goal: the fleet's credits rise. Measured 2026-11-15: mining iron and copper for skill cost about 160 cr per ship-hour, 22,000 cr an hour for the fleet, and the ore has no buyer.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F9. A hunter fights grazers of 100 hull or less, one hunter per place, and flees under 60% hull or when a ship joins the fight. The Military division may test bigger prey and other fits: one ship per test, the flee rule kept, hull and fit written into the route.
  Goal: no ship lost to a hunt.
  Changes when: a hull is tested against bigger prey and its route says so.
F10. The command group is the Fleet Admiral's own division, Command: six flagships (the Fleet Admiral's command ship and one captain ship per Admiral) and their guard, the sentinel- ships. The group flies one plan (the roles command and sentinel). An Admiral lists its flagship in its FLEET.md; the flagship flies with the command group, under the Fleet Admiral's hand.
  Goal: the fleet's command moves as one group under one hand (Suggi, 2026-11-15).
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F11. The faction The Collective (tag CLTV) exists since 2026-11-15. Its leader is the Fleet Admiral's flagship; its members are the command group; its cap reads 50 members. Inviting, joining, leaving, a role, a war, peace, an alliance and every word in the faction's name are the Fleet Admiral's, on Suggi's word. No other pilot of ours joins any faction.
  Goal: one voice toward other players, and a faction that stays ours.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F12. Money. An Admiral spends only the wallets of its own ships and never takes one below F7. On tests (a module, goods for a trade or a craft, a hull) a division spends at most 10% of its ships' credits in one turn; Military 25%, for weapons and hulls. Before goods are bought for a mission, a trade or a craft: one ship, one unit of the test, the answer read. A bid's quantity in our market files (`buy_quantity`) is the total of all its price levels: before a chain is costed or goods are bought for it, read the depth at the best price (`best_buy_qty`, or view_market at the station; errors.log ENT-015). A station's own bid for a few units is a one-time price until data/market/history.jsonl shows it refill (`ledger.py prices <item>`). Nothing is given away, jettisoned or sold under a standing bid to empty a hold or a storage; a mission that counts units sold is the one exception, inside its route.
  Goal: a test that fails costs little. On 2026-11-15 about 92,000 cr went into goods for a mission that hands them out, and three chains were costed on a depth that was a total.
  Changes when: a division shows income that repeats.
F13. Beyond Voidborn space (Suggi, 2026-11-15: we explore all around us, we go hard, it is dangerous, we need to make money). The whole fleet explores the lawless systems around our ten: every division's ships and the whole command group, flagships and sentinels. The work is the route game/routes/census-frontier.json, in every plan before park: a free ship reads whole the system read longest ago and moves on to the next; twelve ships at a time; each system again after four hours. Its list: the 27 systems within two jumps of our border, nearest first, then Atlas and Intercrus, and LHS 1140 for its station; Oracle keeps the list and widens it ring by ring. What a read finds lies in data/scouting/<system>.json and in the system's file in game/planets/, and the route maker turns it into candidates within ten minutes. A route that works beyond policed space carries `requires.empty_hold` (nothing aboard to lose) and, when it hunts, `alone` (no fight where a ship that is not ours stands): the route maker writes both by itself. No ship carries goods of value out; it sells and stores at a Voidborn station. A ship lost is an entry in errors.log with its cause, and the system where it was lost leaves the list until Military has looked at it.
  Goal: find what pays more than our six stations do, and the place where a base of ours could stand.
  Changes when: the reports of the reads.
F14. Knowledge. Each Admiral keeps its guidebook, game/routes/guidebook/<division>.md: the routes that pay with their measured figures, what did not pay and why, how its pilots level, the hull and the fit for its work. Every member reads the others'. A figure comes from routes.py measure or a run's RESULT line, never from memory. What an Admiral means to do next stands in `planned` of its plan file.
  Goal: every ship born later starts where the others stand, and five Admirals do not test the same thing twice.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
F15. Data (Suggi, 2026-11-15: the speed at which what we read becomes information the fleet can use is the key). Everything the fleet reads is kept by itself, by services that run without a turn, and is every member's to read: every ship of every division with its order, place and wallet (`game/fleet/board.py all`); what is in storage and how every price moved (`game/fleet/ledger.py show all`, `prices <item>`); every way to earn that the readings show, costed and with its route file (`game/fleet/routemaker.py show all`); what the routes measured (game/routes/index.md); who our ships met and what others wrote (data/intel/, `game/fleet/inbox.py show`); the Admirals' guidebooks (F14). A figure an Admiral needs twice is a request for a script, not a second hand count.
  Goal: five Admirals plan on the same picture, minutes old.
  Changes when: Suggi's word.
TURN
T7. From this entry until the next. A turn is the four hours between two scheduled runs: the Admirals at 00:00, 04:00, 08:00, 12:00, 16:00 and 20:00 UTC (Zero on the hour, then Apollo, Mercury, Apophis and Oracle five minutes apart), the Fleet Admiral one hour after them. The scheduled runs are off until Suggi's go; a run is started by hand until then. Between two turns the ships fly by themselves from the plans (F8).
  Done when: each Admiral's report names the routes its ships flew with their measured credits per ship-hour, every test it started with its RESULT line, how many of its ships its plan keeps at work for the next four hours, and what the levels of its pilots gained; its plan and its guidebook are committed; none of its ships is left held by hand.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: you command your division's ships; send them out now. Each turn: (1) read the picture (F15): `python3 -I -B game/fleet/division.py me`, `game/fleet/board.py all`, `game/fleet/ledger.py show`, `game/fleet/routemaker.py show`, game/routes/index.md, your guidebook and the others', `game/fleet/inbox.py show --to-us`. (2) Keep your plan a standing loop for all your ships (F8): missions that pay once, what pays with its `max_ships` lifted, the tests, the census, park. (3) Test every candidate worth a test at once, one ship each, in our space and on the frontier: `game/fleet/routemaker.py route <id>` prints a candidate's route file; save it under game/routes/, put it in your plan, run the gate and the index, commit; the dispatcher flies it within the minute. What no runner can fly yet, fly by hand with one ship and ask for the step. (4) After the first RESULT lines: `routes.py measure --write`, lift `max_ships` where the figure holds, take out what lost money. (5) Level your pilots on work that pays: name in your guidebook which skill each route trains and what the next hull or module needs. (6) Write what the tests showed into your guidebook and what you mean to do next into `planned`. (7) Report what your ships did, earned and cost, with fuel and repair named where a record holds them, what failed, and what you need from me: a route, a runner's step, a skill, data. The shared skill fleet-play says how; your FLEET.md lists your ships.
Limits: F1 to F14. The stance of F2 holds for every ship and every test. Your own ships only, and beyond Voidborn space only the systems of F13; what you need from another division is a request in requests.log, and you answer the open requests addressed to you at every session start. You write your plan, the routes your ships fly and your guidebook; a script of game/fleet/, a route another division flies too and another role's plan change by a request. Release every ship you took by hand before your turn ends. What these orders do not cover is a request to me, never a guess.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: mine what sells, with every miner. Water ice is the first mined good with bids of depth: four of our stations bid 25 to 36 cr for thousands of units, and your one run from Wolf 1061 made +2,688 cr in 30 minutes after fuel (your report of 23:05 UTC). What you asked for exists since 23:12 UTC: a mine route sells at the end of every run (`sell` in the route file), game/routes/fit-ice-harvester.json buys and fits the harvester at home (1,413 cr and 1% tax; 22 on offer at our six stations, read 22:40 to 22:58 UTC), and ten ice fields have their route files, written by the route maker from the census (game/routes/mine-*.json: Node Beta Frozen Drift in policed space, nine beyond it), each a test with `max_ships` 1. Put the fit route and the ice routes into your plan now, all at once; four sentinels of the command group fly the same routes as pathfinders. After each first RESULT line lift `max_ships` as far as the bids carry: read `ledger.py prices water_ice` and the depth before you add ships, and watch the nitrogen and deuterium ice that find no bid. Inside F12 a harvester for every miner the routes can carry. Then the next goods: deuterium ice (bids near 200 cr for few units), plasma gas (a gas harvester takes the mining laser's slot), and whatever field the census brings; the route maker lists them. Keep the focused-crystal craft for when its bid stands. No ship mines iron or copper for storage.
Found 2026-11-15: water ice from Wolf 1061 sold at Synchrony, +2,688 cr in 30.1 minutes after 195 cr of fuel, one run (game/routes/guidebook/mining.md, section 6); the focused-crystal bid is one unit; Ironhollow's belt read 47,201 iron ore and 99,999 copper ore, which nobody buys; Acubens Belt read 41 carbon ore again at 22:17 UTC.
Measure: credits per ship-hour of your best route after all costs; the number of your ships on a route that pays.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find a trade that pays and holds, and bring the fleet its one-time credits. The route maker lists every gap between two of our books with the depth read level by level (`routemaker.py show --kind trade`): at 23:13 UTC the one gap that carries a full load pays an estimated 83 cr per ship-hour (iridium ore bought at 1 cr and sold at 2 cr) and every richer gap closes after one to five units, so the trade that pays lies at a station we have not read. The census now reads Antares and LHS 1140 (your request): when their books are in data/market/, cost the best gap, test it with one ship and a few units, hold empty on the way out (F13). Until then your traders earn with what pays in the fleet: ask Zero for the ice routes once their first RESULT lines are read (a harvester costs 1,427 cr), and fly every route that pays once: game/routes/voidborn-precedence.json (4,000 cr), voidborn-selling.json (for the pilots that hold Market Participation: Selling) and the three voidborn-first-hunt routes are tests of the command group now; put them into your plan when their RESULT lines are read. Keep the control-node craft by hand for when its bids stand; the craft step of the runner is still owed to you.
Found 2026-11-15: no gap between two of our stations that is worth a trip holds with depth (your turns T5 and T6; the route maker's read of 23:13 UTC); Concurrent Approval pays about 2,950 cr once per pilot and buys nothing.
Measure: credits per ship-hour from buying and selling; one-time credits collected.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: carry what sells, and keep the fleet's goods. The ore in storage stays: no bid takes it with depth, and steel and wiring are what a base will need. Your haulers earn with the fleet's paying routes until hauling has its own: put the routes that pay once into your plan as their RESULT lines come (voidborn-precedence, voidborn-selling, the voidborn-first-hunt routes), and the ice routes with Zero once their figures hold (the hold of a starter is the same 65 units). Name what the ice routes leave in storage without a buyer (nitrogen, deuterium, methane ice) and where it finds one: a station beyond our space, a recipe. Say which station is the fleet's storage home and what a faction lockbox needs (200 steel and 50 circuits, 200,000 cr: the game's base guide) from what we store. Carry what another division asks for in requests.log. The craft step of the runner is still owed to you.
Found 2026-11-15: crafting from storage works and costs no credits (your hand chain of turn T6); no bid in our space takes ore or what ore makes with depth; a starter burns 1 unit of fuel per jump and 1 per flight inside a system (your measure over 87 jumps).
Measure: credits recovered from stored goods; requests of other divisions carried out.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt, explore with the scouts, and design the ships for battle. Keep one hunter on each veil-ray herd of our space, ranched. Beyond our space five veil-ray herds have their route files, written by the route maker (game/routes/hunt-*-veil-ray.json: Cedarhold, Garnet, Okab, Achird, Keid): each a test with one ship, `alone` (the hunter looks before every fight and leaves where a ship that is not ours stands) and `requires.empty_hold`; put them into your plan now, all at once, and add Ruchbah again with `alone`. The three voidborn-first-hunt routes (1,000 cr once and 50 Weapons XP a pilot, the hunt step you asked for) are tests of the command group now: into your plan for every drone when their RESULT lines are read. A hunt row records the hull before, after and at its lowest; a hand-in by hand is booked with `gate.py goal <pilot> complete-mission ... --one-time "<title>"`. Go on with the railgun and the fits, then name the hull and the fit the division flies next, what it costs and what it earns. Where our miners and scouts work beyond the border, a drone at the same place is their guard by being there: it reports who comes; a pirate or a pilot is fought only when it fired first (F2), and who fired is reported the same turn.
Found 2026-11-15: the two veil-ray herds of our space pay about 3,100 and 3,350 cr per hunter-hour; the herd at Ruchbah was taken over by two ships of a large faction within three minutes of our first fight, and our hunter fled as built; First Hunt paid +922 cr after fuel in 25 minutes, by hand, twice.
Measure: credits per hunter-hour; ships lost, which stays 0.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: lead the reading of the frontier (F13) and keep the picture fresh. game/routes/census-frontier.json is yours: the list, how often, how many ships at once. It holds 30 systems now; widen it ring by ring toward the nearest stations of other factions, and take a system off where a ship was lost. Every read becomes candidates in the route maker within ten minutes: say in queue.log what a read found that no script can see. For each system say whether a base could stand there (lawless, a place without a station; GOAL). Watch who is in our space: the larger groups of one faction per system are on the command station's map and in data/station/; the faction files are in game/factions/. Read `game/fleet/inbox.py show` at every turn: what other pilots write near our ships is yours to judge, and a message to us is the Fleet Admiral's to answer. File every pilot that fired first at a ship of ours as `h-` in the same turn (F2). Keep every Voidborn system read within the hour. The corrections of game/rules/ for releases 0.715.3 to 0.717.0 are still owed to you (requests.log ENT-038).
Found 2026-11-15: 18 frontier systems read by 22:04 UTC, none with a station; every home system read within the hour; the factions most seen in our space in 24 hours were OLSV, IRLC, HIVE and BUSY (the public jump stream, 22:23 UTC), and our own ships had met pilots of each of them by 19:07 UTC, the first reading of the service that records it.
Measure: systems beyond Voidborn space read; the age of the oldest Voidborn read.

